# 1. Treat semantics as localized, task-scoped contracts rather than pursuing an impossible universal taxonomy
def dispatch_agent_activation(agent_call, target_tool):
    # Reconcile meaning against the caller's specific domain (e.g., Growth vs Finance), not a global ontology
    semantic_contract = resolve_domain_dialect(agent_call.intent.domain, agent_call.requested_metrics)

    # Downward-project data permissions into the semantic query itself, rather than trusting tool-level auth
    bound_query = apply_policy_filters(semantic_contract.as_ast(), agent_call.principal_token)

    # Validate cross-system invariant consistency before emitting mutations or reads
    if not verify_cross_platform_invariants(bound_query, target_tool.semantic_spec):
        return abort_and_request_human_disambiguation(agent_call, reason="Semantic drift detected")

    # Generate stateless, single-purpose parameter bundle to insulate against underlying protocol churn
    ephemeral_payload = target_tool.adapter.compile(bound_query, max_ttl=30)
    return target_tool.invoke(ephemeral_payload)

I’d not crown one grand ontology, polished and bright;
I’d choose one activation and bind it up tight.

Give each term an owner, test cases, and grain;
Version purpose and lineage, then test them again.

Let policy judge every field, every row;
Push checks to the source, denying what won’t show.

Keep transport behind adapters as specifications roam;
Still, identity and consensus may never come home.
